# CLOUD & SYSTEMS — core
add('cloud-url-journey','cloud','Networking','What Actually Happens When You Type a URL?','A browser request crosses DNS, local networking, routing, transport security, HTTP, application logic, and rendering before a page appears.',
'''Typing a URL starts a chain of cooperating systems. The browser first interprets the URL and looks for cached information. If it needs an address, the operating system and a DNS resolver work to map the hostname to an IP address. The host then decides whether the destination is local or remote and, for a remote network, sends traffic toward its default gateway.\n\nFor HTTPS, the client establishes a transport connection and then negotiates TLS so the server can prove its identity and both sides can protect the session. Only then does the HTTP request carry application-level intent such as “GET /”. The response may reference more resources—CSS, JavaScript, fonts, images—causing additional requests before the browser can construct and paint the page.''',
'It connects topics that are often learned separately. DNS, ARP, routing, TCP, TLS, HTTP, load balancing, servers, and browsers are not isolated facts; they are stages in one system.',
'If DNS fails, the browser may never learn where to send the request. If DNS succeeds but TCP/443 is blocked, the hostname resolves correctly while the secure connection still fails. Different symptoms reveal different layers.',
'Troubleshooting becomes faster when you ask “Which stage failed?” instead of treating “the website is down” as one undifferentiated problem.',
minutes=11,tags=['dns','tcp','tls','http','routing','browser'],interactive='urljourney',related=['cloud-dns-resolution','cloud-arp','cyber-tls'],quiz={'q':'If a hostname resolves to the correct IP but HTTPS times out, which conclusion is safest?','options':['DNS must be broken','Name resolution worked; investigate transport, firewall, routing, or server availability','The browser cache is definitely corrupt','The MAC address cannot be learned'], 'answer':1,'explanation':'A correct DNS answer shows name resolution succeeded. The failure can still occur later in the path.'},objectives=['Trace a web request across major layers','Use symptoms to narrow a failure domain'])
add('cloud-dns-resolution','cloud','Networking','DNS Resolution, Step by Step','DNS is a distributed naming system that turns names people use into records computers can act on.',
'''A stub resolver on your device normally asks a recursive resolver for an answer. If the recursive resolver has a valid cached result, it can answer immediately. Otherwise it follows the DNS hierarchy: root servers point toward a top-level domain, the TLD points toward authoritative name servers, and an authoritative server returns the requested record.\n\nCaching is essential. Each record carries a time-to-live (TTL) that tells resolvers how long the answer may be reused. This reduces latency and global query load, but it also means changes may not appear everywhere at once.''',
'DNS is a dependency for Active Directory, cloud services, web applications, mail, service discovery, and most user-facing network activity.',
'A client can ping 1.1.1.1 but cannot open example.com. That pattern suggests IP connectivity may exist while name resolution is failing.',
'DNS answers “what name maps to what data?”; routing answers “how do packets reach that destination?” They solve different problems.',
minutes=9,tags=['dns','ttl','resolver','authoritative'],interactive='dns',related=['cloud-url-journey','cloud-dhcp'],quiz={'q':'What is the main purpose of a DNS TTL?','options':['Encrypt a DNS response','Limit how long a cached record may be reused','Select the default gateway','Measure network latency'],'answer':1,'explanation':'TTL controls cache lifetime; it does not encrypt traffic or choose routes.'},misconception='DNS does not “carry” your web traffic. It usually helps your client learn addressing information used by later network connections.',objectives=['Distinguish recursive and authoritative DNS','Explain why caching changes troubleshooting'])
add('cloud-subnetting','cloud','Networking','Subnetting Without Memorization','A subnet prefix divides an IP address into network and host portions. Understanding the binary boundary is more durable than memorizing tables.',
'''IPv4 addresses contain 32 bits. A /24 prefix means the first 24 bits identify the network, leaving 8 bits for addresses inside that subnet. Increasing the prefix length borrows bits from the host portion, creating smaller subnets.\n\nFor traditional IPv4 subnets, a network address identifies the subnet and the all-ones host value is the broadcast address. The number of addresses is 2^(32-prefix). Many conventional LAN calculations subtract two for network and broadcast, although some special point-to-point and platform-specific uses differ.''',
'Subnetting determines which destinations are considered directly reachable, where routing is required, how broadcast domains are bounded, and how address space is allocated.',
'192.168.10.0/26 contains 64 addresses: .0 through .63. In a conventional LAN, .0 is the network address, .63 is broadcast, and .1–.62 are usable host addresses.',
'A prefix is not merely a capacity number; it changes the network boundary that every host uses to decide “local or routed?”',
minutes=12,tags=['ipv4','cidr','routing','networking'],interactive='subnet',related=['cloud-arp','cloud-vlan'],quiz={'q':'How many total IPv4 addresses are in a /26?','options':['32','62','64','128'],'answer':2,'explanation':'A /26 leaves 6 host bits, so 2^6 = 64 total addresses.'},objectives=['Calculate basic IPv4 subnet ranges','Connect prefixes to routing behavior'])
add('cloud-linux-permissions','cloud','Linux','Linux Permissions: Read, Write, Execute','Linux mode bits express permissions for an owner, a group, and everyone else.',
'''Traditional Unix permissions are grouped into three triplets: user, group, and other. Each triplet can grant read (4), write (2), and execute (1). Adding the values gives the familiar numeric form: 7 means rwx, 6 means rw-, 5 means r-x, and so on.\n\nFor files, read allows content access, write allows modification, and execute allows the file to be run when it is an executable format. For directories, the meanings shift: read lists names, write changes directory entries, and execute allows traversal. Ownership and group membership therefore matter just as much as the numeric mode.''',
'Permission problems are a common cause of service failures, deployment mistakes, and accidental exposure. Understanding the model is safer than repeatedly applying chmod 777 until something works.',
'A configuration file with mode 640 gives the owner read/write, the group read-only access, and others no access: rw-r-----.',
'Use the least permission needed. “It works” and “it is appropriately protected” are separate tests.',
minutes=9,tags=['linux','chmod','ownership','security'],interactive='permissions',related=['cyber-least-privilege','cloud-systemd'],quiz={'q':'What does mode 640 mean?','options':['Owner rw, group r, others none','Everyone rw','Owner rwx, group r, others none','Owner r, group w, others none'],'answer':0,'explanation':'6=rw-, 4=r--, 0=---.'},misconception='chmod 777 is not a general troubleshooting solution; it grants broad access and can create a security problem.',objectives=['Decode numeric permissions','Explain directory execute permission'])
add('cloud-recovery-rto-rpo','cloud','Recovery','RTO and RPO: Two Different Recovery Targets','RTO describes how quickly service should return; RPO describes how much data loss the business can tolerate.',
'''Recovery Time Objective (RTO) is a target for elapsed time from disruption to restoration of an acceptable service level. Recovery Point Objective (RPO) is a target for how far back in time recovered data may be. They answer different questions.\n\nA four-hour RTO and fifteen-minute RPO imply different engineering needs: rapid restoration procedures for the first target and sufficiently frequent or continuous data protection for the second. Neither objective proves that recovery will succeed. Restore tests, dependency mapping, credentials, documentation, and people are part of recovery readiness.''',
'Backup schedules are often discussed without connecting them to business impact. RTO and RPO translate business tolerance into engineering constraints.',
'If payroll can tolerate at most 15 minutes of lost transactions but can be unavailable for two hours, its RPO is tighter than its RTO.',
'Backups are evidence only when they can be restored. Recovery is a tested process, not a checkbox.',
minutes=8,tags=['backup','rto','rpo','disaster recovery'],related=['leadership-incident-command'],quiz={'q':'Which target primarily constrains acceptable data loss?','options':['RTO','RPO','MTTR only','SLA only'],'answer':1,'explanation':'RPO expresses the acceptable recovery point in time and therefore tolerated data loss.'},objectives=['Differentiate RTO from RPO','Translate recovery targets into technical implications'])

# cloud quick concepts
quick_cloud=[
('cloud-arp','Networking','ARP: IP-to-MAC Resolution','ARP helps an IPv4 host learn the link-layer address associated with a local IPv4 address.','When an IPv4 destination is on the local subnet, the sender needs a destination MAC address for the Ethernet frame. ARP requests ask who owns an IP; the owner replies with its MAC. For remote destinations, the host usually resolves the MAC of its default gateway—not the remote server.','ARP operates on the local broadcast domain. It does not route packets across the Internet.',['arp','mac','ethernet']),
('cloud-dhcp','Networking','DHCP: More Than an IP Address','DHCP can supply an address, subnet mask or prefix information, a default gateway, DNS servers, lease timing, and other options.','A typical DHCP exchange lets a client discover available configuration, request an offered lease, and receive acknowledgement. Centralized lease management reduces manual addressing errors and allows administrators to change options without touching every endpoint.','A working IP address with a wrong DNS option can create a partial outage that looks very different from having no lease at all.',['dhcp','ip','dns']),
('cloud-nat','Networking','NAT: Address Translation at a Boundary','Network Address Translation rewrites address information as traffic crosses a translating device.','The common home/enterprise edge case maps many private IPv4 hosts to one or a few public addresses, usually also tracking transport ports. NAT conserves public IPv4 space and hides internal addressing, but it is not the same thing as a firewall policy.','NAT changes addressing; a firewall makes an allow/deny decision. A device can perform both functions, which is why the concepts are often conflated.',['nat','ipv4','firewall']),
('cloud-vlan','Networking','VLANs: One Switch, Multiple Layer-2 Domains','A VLAN logically separates Layer-2 broadcast domains on switching infrastructure.','Access ports normally carry one VLAN for an endpoint, while trunk links can carry traffic for multiple VLANs using tagging. Communication between VLANs requires Layer-3 routing somewhere in the design.','A VLAN is segmentation at Layer 2. It does not automatically create a security policy between networks.',['vlan','switching','802.1q']),
('cloud-systemd','Linux','systemd: Services and System State','systemd is the init and service-management system used by many Linux distributions.','Units describe services, mounts, timers, sockets, targets, and other managed resources. Commands such as systemctl status, start, stop, enable, and journalctl help administrators inspect both service state and logs.','“Enabled” means configured to start under defined conditions; it does not necessarily mean the service is running right now.',['linux','systemd','journalctl']),
]
